## Output discipline

Klaviyo and Shopify endpoints return verbose JSON. A naive `return campaigns.data` for 50 campaigns can be 150KB+; the host caps responses at ~100KB and replaces the rest with a `{truncated: true, ...}` envelope. Use these helpers — available as globals in the sandbox — to project, rank, and aggregate before returning.

### pick(value, schema)

Projection over arbitrarily-deep objects / arrays. Schema mirrors the value's shape; truthy leaves are kept.

```js
// Flat projection.
const trimmed = pick({id: 'abc', name: 'Welcome', body: '...10KB of HTML...'}, {id: true, name: true});
// → { id: 'abc', name: 'Welcome' }

// Nested projection.
const { data } = await klaviyo.campaigns.list({
  filter: 'equals(messages.channel,"email")',
  'page[size]': '50'
});
return pick(data, { id: true, attributes: { name: true, status: true, send_time: true } });
```

### topN(arr, n, by)

Top N items by a numeric key (descending). `by` is a string property name OR a function returning a number.

```js
const report = await klaviyo.reporting.campaignValues({ ...body });
const results = report.data.attributes.results;
return topN(results, 5, (r) => r.statistics.conversion_value);
```

### summarize(arr, opts)

Auto-aggregate. Returns `{ count, total?, min?, max?, avg?, top? }`.

```js
const rows = await shopify.ql('FROM sales SHOW gross_sales GROUP BY day SINCE -30d UNTIL today');
return summarize(rows.rows, {
  by: (r) => parseFloat(r.gross_sales),
  topN: 3,
});
// → { count: 30, total: 145200, min: 1200, max: 9800, avg: 4840, top: [...top 3 by gross_sales] }
```

### When the cap fires

If you ever see `{truncated: true, originalBytes: N, instructions: ...}` in your result, your code returned too much. Re-run with `pick` to narrow fields, or `topN` / `summarize` to aggregate. The cap defaults to 100KB and can be overridden by the MCP operator via `DTC_MCP_MAX_RESPONSE_KB`.

### Why this exists

LLMs running in code-execution sandboxes consistently over-return — published benchmarks put the factuality ceiling at ~53% across all code-mode MCPs because models 'tend toward verbose responses beyond what's strictly necessary.' These helpers + the host cap directly attack that ceiling.

## execute_code sandbox

Write an async function body. Use `return` to return a value to the MCP caller. Globals: `klaviyo`, `shopify`, `console`, plus the output-discipline helpers `pick`, `topN`, `summarize` (see `guide.output-discipline`). No `fetch`/`process`/`require`/`import`. Default wall-clock: 30s; opt-in via `// @timeout 2m` at the top (max 5m).

```js
// @timeout 1m
const metricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();
const lists = await klaviyo.lists.list({ 'page[size]': '100' });
return { metricId, listCount: lists.data.length };
```

All Klaviyo calls go through the host's rate limiter (10/s standard, 1/s reporting). All Shopify GraphQL calls go through Shopify's cost budget. Reporting POSTs and ShopifyQL queries are cached server-side; repeat calls within ~5–10 minutes don't re-hit the API.

**Response cap:** the host truncates any return value larger than ~100KB and prepends a `{truncated: true, instructions: ...}` envelope. To stay under the cap, project your data with `pick`/`topN`/`summarize` before returning. See `guide.output-discipline`.

## Recipe: full DTC health dashboard

This replaces the old `dtc_dashboard` tool. Run both API surfaces in parallel via `Promise.all`.

```js
// @timeout 1m
const days = 30;
const metricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();

const [sales, campaignReport, flowReport] = await Promise.all([
  shopify.ql(`FROM sales SHOW gross_sales, net_sales, orders SINCE -${days}d UNTIL today`),
  klaviyo.reporting.campaignValues({
    data: { type: 'campaign-values-report', attributes: {
      timeframe: { key: 'last_30_days' },
      conversion_metric_id: metricId,
      statistics: ['conversion_value', 'recipients', 'open_rate'],
    }}
  }),
  klaviyo.reporting.flowValues({
    data: { type: 'flow-values-report', attributes: {
      timeframe: { key: 'last_30_days' },
      conversion_metric_id: metricId,
      statistics: ['conversion_value', 'recipients'],
    }}
  }),
]);

const row = sales.rows[0] || {};
const totalRevenue = parseFloat(row.net_sales || '0');
const orders = parseInt(row.orders || '0');

const campaignRevenue = (campaignReport.data.attributes.results || [])
  .reduce((s, r) => s + (r.statistics.conversion_value || 0), 0);
const flowRevenue = (flowReport.data.attributes.results || [])
  .reduce((s, r) => s + (r.statistics.conversion_value || 0), 0);

return {
  period_days: days,
  sales: { revenue: totalRevenue, orders, aov: orders ? totalRevenue / orders : 0 },
  email: {
    total_email_revenue: campaignRevenue + flowRevenue,
    pct_of_total: totalRevenue ? ((campaignRevenue + flowRevenue) / totalRevenue) * 100 : 0,
    campaign_revenue: campaignRevenue,
    flow_revenue: flowRevenue,
  },
};
```

```js
const metricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();
const report = await klaviyo.reporting.campaignValues({
  data: { type: 'campaign-values-report', attributes: {
    timeframe: { key: 'last_30_days' },
    conversion_metric_id: metricId,
    statistics: ['recipients', 'opens_unique', 'open_rate', 'clicks_unique', 'click_rate', 'conversion_value'],
  }}
});

const rows = (report.data.attributes.results || [])
  .map(r => ({
    campaign_id: r.groupings.campaign_id,
    recipients: r.statistics.recipients,
    open_rate: r.statistics.open_rate,
    click_rate: r.statistics.click_rate,
    revenue: r.statistics.conversion_value,
  }))
  .sort((a, b) => b.revenue - a.revenue)
  .slice(0, 10);

// Hydrate names (only for the top 10 — saves rate-limit budget)
for (const row of rows) {
  const { data } = await klaviyo.campaigns.get(row.campaign_id, {
    'fields[campaign]': 'name,send_time',
  });
  row.name = data.attributes.name;
  row.send_time = data.attributes.send_time;
}
return rows;
```

If `SHOPIFY_STORE` (plus either client credentials or access token) is not set, all `shopify.*` calls throw with a configuration error. `klaviyo.*` calls still work. Check configuration availability before calling Shopify:

```js
try {
  const tz = await shopify.timezone();
} catch (e) {
  // Shopify not configured — fall back to Klaviyo-only analysis
}
```

## Stateful sandbox sessions

The sandbox keeps a **single context alive for the lifetime of your MCP connection**. Variables you assign to `globalThis` in one `execute_code` call are visible in every subsequent call within the same conversation. This is intentional and is one of the architectural differences from Stainless (which runs each call in a fresh Cloudflare Worker isolate — stateless).

For multi-step DTC analyses (a typical workflow: fetch campaigns → drill into a specific one → cross-reference Shopify orders), this means:
- The first call fetches and caches expensive data into `globalThis`.
- Subsequent calls reference those values directly without re-fetching.
- The host's reporting cache STILL handles repeat API calls within the same session — statefulness compounds with it.

### Sharing data: use globalThis

Because user code runs as a fresh script per call, top-level `const` / `let` declarations are **scoped to that call only** — they're NOT visible later. Use `globalThis` (or `globalThis.x = ...` shorthand) for anything you want to carry forward.

```js
// Call 1
const metricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();
globalThis.metricId = metricId;  // persisted
const topCampaigns = topN(
  (await klaviyo.reporting.campaignValues({
    data: { type: 'campaign-values-report', attributes: {
      timeframe: { key: 'last_30_days' },
      conversion_metric_id: metricId,
      statistics: ['conversion_value', 'recipients']
    }}
  })).data.attributes.results,
  5,
  (r) => r.statistics.conversion_value
);
globalThis.topCampaigns = topCampaigns;  // persisted
return pick(topCampaigns, { groupings: { campaign_id: true }, statistics: { conversion_value: true } });
```

```js
// Call 2 — no re-fetch needed
const topIds = globalThis.topCampaigns.map(c => c.groupings.campaign_id);
const details = await Promise.all(topIds.map(id =>
  klaviyo.campaigns.get(id, { 'fields[campaign]': 'name,send_time' })
));
return details.map(d => ({ id: d.data.id, name: d.data.attributes.name }));
```

### Session reset (TTL + memory)

The context is recreated when any of the following happens:
- The MCP connection closes (Claude Desktop is quit or the extension is disabled)
- 30 minutes of idle time pass with no `execute_code` call (configurable via env)
- Memory usage exceeds the per-isolate cap (256 MB)
- The user manually reloads the extension

When the context is recreated, the next call's response includes `sessionReset: true` and a `sessionResetNote`. If you see that, any globals you set previously are gone — re-declare what you need.

```js
// You can detect a reset by checking globalThis.
if (typeof globalThis.cachedMetricId === 'undefined') {
  globalThis.cachedMetricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();
}
const metricId = globalThis.cachedMetricId;
```

### Why this beats Stainless's stateless model

Stainless's Cloudflare-Workers sandbox runs each `execute` in a fresh isolate. For iterative analyses they require re-fetching at every step, which inflates token cost AND duration. Our self-hosted sidecar trivially supports state because it owns the isolate lifecycle.

## klaviyo.campaigns.get(id, params?)

Fetch one campaign. Use `include=campaign-messages,tags` to pull related entities in one call.

```js
const { data, included } = await klaviyo.campaigns.get('01H...', {
  include: 'campaign-messages',
  'fields[campaign-message]': 'label,content',
});
```

## klaviyo.campaigns.list(params?)

See https://developers.klaviyo.com/en/reference/get_campaigns. Filter syntax required — at minimum a `filter` param scoping to a channel.

```js
const { data } = await klaviyo.campaigns.list({
  filter: 'equals(messages.channel,"email")',
  sort: '-send_time',
  'page[size]': '20',
  'fields[campaign]': 'name,status,send_time',
});
return data.map(c => ({ id: c.id, ...c.attributes }));
```

## klaviyo.events.list(params?)

Filter by `metric_id` to scope to one metric (e.g. Placed Order).

```js
const metricId = await klaviyo.getConversionMetricId();
const { data } = await klaviyo.events.list({
  filter: `equals(metric_id,"${metricId}")`,
  sort: '-datetime',
  'page[size]': '50',
  include: 'profile',
});
```

## klaviyo.flows.get(id, params?)

```js
const { data, included } = await klaviyo.flows.get('Vd...', {
  include: 'flow-actions',
});
```

